From bec9b4e0107ab3105a939b2cee0a9ee1f5d8d821 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GENESIS_OS Contributor <genesis-os-bot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 6 Jul 2026 11:31:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GEN-0025 - Storage & Dependency Injection Hardening

Objective: make the Storage subsystem resilient and driver-independent at
startup, without adding functionality, changing repository interfaces, or
touching Agent/Workflow Runtime.

Root cause confirmed empirically (see tests/unit/storage/test_database.py's
new driver-blocking regression test): SQLAlchemy's create_async_engine()
resolves and imports the URL's DBAPI driver module (aiosqlite, asyncpg)
immediately when called -- constructing a DatabaseSessionManager therefore
required the corresponding driver package to be importable even if no
database operation was ever attempted. di_wire.wire_storage() always
builds a DatabaseSessionManager as part of assembling the
RepositoryRegistry, so this coupling reached every caller of
wire_storage(), including ones that only wanted the in-memory
tasks/events/memory repositories.

src/storage/database.py:
- DatabaseSessionManager: engine + session factory creation deferred from
  __init__ to first actual use (session()/create_all()/dispose()), via a
  new private _ensure_engine() that builds and memoizes both. Public API
  (constructor signature, all method signatures) and async behavior are
  unchanged; the driver is still required the moment anything actually
  touches the database, exactly as before.
- dispose() is now a true no-op if the engine was never created, rather
  than constructing one just to immediately tear it down.
- Added a small private _backend_name() helper for a new log field
  (backend=...) on engine creation -- diagnostic only.

src/storage/di_wire.py: reviewed per the milestone's requirements; no
change was needed. With DatabaseSessionManager's engine creation now
lazy, wire_storage()'s existing dependency graph already builds
successfully regardless of driver availability, and the in-memory
tasks/events/memory registry entries were already unconditional inside
_build_registry -- both keep working independently of the SQL/Redis/
Qdrant backends without any wiring changes.

src/config/settings.py: added Settings.database_backend, a read-only
computed property deriving the backend name (e.g. "sqlite",
"postgresql") from the existing database_url field. Purely additive --
no new field, no serialization change, no effect on how
DatabaseSessionManager resolves the dialect (still the full URL,
unchanged). Deliberately not shared code with database.py's own
_backend_name() helper, so src/storage/ never needs to import
src/config/ for this.

Tests: 9 new/updated (4 in test_database.py including the driver-blocking
regression test, 1 new case in test_di_wire.py proving wire_storage()
itself is driver-independent, 4 new in tests/unit/config/test_settings.py
for database_backend). All existing tests pass unmodified in behavior
(test_database.py's two _safe_url tests, previously fragile because they
implicitly required asyncpg to be installed just to construct a
DatabaseSessionManager, now pass for the right reason).

Verified: ruff check, ruff format --check, mypy --strict src (59 files),
pytest (210 passed, up from 201; 96% coverage, database.py at 100%) all
clean. src/core/ and src/runtime/ (Agent Runtime + Workflow Runtime) diffs
are both empty.
